  "interview": {
    "expected_score": 40,
    "expected_score_probability": 50,
    "full_score_probability": 10,
    "hardest_part": "Steering at a one-second decision cadence. At rally speed the car covers a lot of ground per held input, so any turn I judge from a single still frame is likely to be entered late or clipped, and the car keeps moving while I think, so corrections arrive after the fact. Driving the full route without accumulating lost time or an unrecoverable pile-up against scenery is the core difficulty; each stage needs all 19 ordered checkpoint passages plus the finish gate, so one bad sector can void an entire stage's 20 points.",
    "likely_failure": "Mid-stage in Stage 1 or 2: a fast corner or crest where I misjudge the road direction from one frame, run off into trees or a barrier, spend many turns reversing and re-aligning, and burn the clock. Secondary failure: losing so much wall-clock time to per-turn latency that I only ever finish one or two stages even though my driving is adequate.",
    "helpful_mechanics": "Automatic transmission removes gear management. Top-down fixed camera makes the road, my heading and upcoming corners legible without camera control. Automatic recovery rescues me from being stuck or pointing the wrong way without spending inputs on a reset menu. Checkpoints are ordered and evenly spread, so partial credit accrues smoothly and each pass is a discrete reward. Result screens only need Enter, and result navigation is enabled, so no progress is lost between stages. Crashes carry no run-ending penalty, so persistence is enough.",
    "harmful_mechanics": "Wide throttle in a low-grip rally car plus a 1-second minimum input duration makes precise corner entry nearly impossible; steering while braking is essential but I get only coarse binary steering. Narrow stages with dense roadside obstacles punish small errors hard. The clock runs during my reasoning, so every frame of deliberation costs route position. No in-race pause, manual recovery or restart means I cannot stop to study a corner or retry a sector. Surface and elevation cues (gravel grip, jumps, blind crests) are harder to read from a still frame than from motion."
  }
